research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/thm-substitution.md-912-
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/thm-substitution.md-913-[L1] If $u$ is integrable on $[a,b]$ with values in $[m,M]$ and $\varphi$ is continuous on $[m,M]$, then $\varphi\circ u$ is integrable ([[thm-composition-with-a-continuous-function]]); an integrable function is bounded, so such $m$ and $M$ exist ([[def-darboux-sums]], [[def-bounded-set]], [[def-interval]]).
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/thm-substitution.md-914-
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/thm-substitution.md:915:[L2] Sums and scalar multiples of integrable functions are integrable, with $\int_a^b(\lambda u + \nu w) = \lambda\int_a^b u + \nu\int_a^b w$ ([[thm-linearity-of-the-integral]]).
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/thm-substitution.md-916-
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/thm-substitution.md-917-[L3] If $u \le w$ pointwise on $[a,b]$ and both are integrable then $\int_a^b u \le \int_a^b w$ ([[thm-monotonicity-of-the-integral]]).
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/thm-substitution.md-918-
--
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/thm-substitution.md-952-
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/thm-substitution.md-953-## Remarks
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/thm-substitution.md-954-
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/thm-substitution.md:955:- **Every integrability clause comes from one theorem plus linearity.** The only input that produces integrability is [[thm-composition-with-a-continuous-function]], with [[thm-linearity-of-the-integral]] recombining the pieces; claim 3 additionally uses [[thm-monotonicity-of-the-integral]], which is the one place an inequality between integrals is needed. The identities of [L5] are algebra, and they are what turns a statement about composing with $|\cdot|$ and $(\cdot)^2$ into statements about products and lattice operations. In particular no new estimate on Darboux sums is made here.
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/thm-substitution.md-956-
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/thm-substitution.md-957-- **The polarisation identity is used, and it is why $f^2$ comes first.** There is no direct route from integrability of $f$ and of $g$ to integrability of $fg$ through the composition theorem, because $(s,t) \mapsto st$ is a function of two variables and the theorem composes with one. Writing $fg$ through squares of sums and differences reduces it to the one-variable case.
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/thm-substitution.md-958-
--
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-refuter-ex-polynomial-integrals-by-the-ftc.prompt.md-167-
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-refuter-ex-polynomial-integrals-by-the-ftc.prompt.md-168-- **The same computation from the definition is a different exercise.** The previous page's companion evaluates $\int_0^1 x^{2}$ from Darboux sums on uniform partitions and a closed form for $\sum_{k<n}\iota(k)^{2}$; that is the value $1/3$, which is $1/\iota(3)$ and agrees with the formula above at $m = 2$. The point of computing by the fundamental theorem is that no closed form for a power sum is needed at any $m$.
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-refuter-ex-polynomial-integrals-by-the-ftc.prompt.md-169-
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-refuter-ex-polynomial-integrals-by-the-ftc.prompt.md:170:- **Linearity extends this to every polynomial.** For $P(x) = \sum_{j<n}a_jx^{j}$ one gets $\int_0^1 P = \sum_{j<n}a_j/\iota(j+1)$ from [[thm-linearity-of-the-integral]]; that is a routine consequence and is not stated as a separate claim.
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-refuter-ex-polynomial-integrals-by-the-ftc.prompt.md-171-````
