research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/cex-the-first-mean-value-theorem-needs-a-one-signed-weight.md-843-
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/cex-the-first-mean-value-theorem-needs-a-one-signed-weight.md-844-## Remarks
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/cex-the-first-mean-value-theorem-needs-a-one-signed-weight.md-845-
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/cex-the-first-mean-value-theorem-needs-a-one-signed-weight.md:846:- **Every integrability clause comes from one theorem plus linearity.** The only input that produces integrability is [[thm-composition-with-a-continuous-function]], with [[thm-linearity-of-the-integral]] recombining the pieces; claim 3 additionally uses [[thm-monotonicity-of-the-integral]], which is the one place an inequality between integrals is needed. The identities of [L5] are algebra, and they are what turns a statement about composing with $|\cdot|$ and $(\cdot)^2$ into statements about products and lattice operations. In particular no new estimate on Darboux sums is made here.
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/cex-the-first-mean-value-theorem-needs-a-one-signed-weight.md-847-
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/cex-the-first-mean-value-theorem-needs-a-one-signed-weight.md-848-- **The polarisation identity is used, and it is why $f^2$ comes first.** There is no direct route from integrability of $f$ and of $g$ to integrability of $fg$ through the composition theorem, because $(s,t) \mapsto st$ is a function of two variables and the theorem composes with one. Writing $fg$ through squares of sums and differences reduces it to the one-variable case.
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/cex-the-first-mean-value-theorem-needs-a-one-signed-weight.md-849-
--
