research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/ex-additivity-on-a-step-function.md-2061-[L4] Finite sums: monotonicity in the terms, scaling, additivity, and splitting; consequently, if $x_i = 0$ for every $i < n$ except $i = j$, then $\sum_{i<n}x_i = x_j$, by splitting at $j$ and at $j+1$ and $\sum 0 = 0$ ([[def-finite-sum]], [[lem-finite-sum-laws]], clauses 1 to 4).
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/ex-additivity-on-a-step-function.md-2062-
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/ex-additivity-on-a-step-function.md:2063:[L5] Sums and scalar multiples of integrable functions are integrable, with the corresponding identity for the integrals ([[thm-linearity-of-the-integral]]); and the constant function $0$ is integrable with integral $0$ ([[lem-integral-elementary-bounds]]).
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/ex-additivity-on-a-step-function.md-2064-
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/ex-additivity-on-a-step-function.md-2065-[L6] For every real $\eta > 0$ there is a natural $N \ge 1$ with $1/\iota(N) < \eta$, and $\iota(N) > 0$ for $N \ge 1$ ([[cor-archimedean-reciprocal]], [[thm-of-archimedean]], [[lem-of-naturals-positive]]).
