research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/ex-polynomial-integrals-by-the-ftc.md-110-- **The same computation from the definition is a different exercise.** The previous page's companion evaluates $\int_0^1 x^{2}$ from Darboux sums on uniform partitions and a closed form for $\sum_{k<n}\iota(k)^{2}$; that is the value $1/3$, which is $1/\iota(3)$ and agrees with the formula above at $m = 2$. The point of computing by the fundamental theorem is that no closed form for a power sum is needed at any $m$.
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/ex-polynomial-integrals-by-the-ftc.md-111-
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/ex-polynomial-integrals-by-the-ftc.md:112:- **Linearity extends this to every polynomial.** For $P(x) = \sum_{j<n}a_jx^{j}$ one gets $\int_0^1 P = \sum_{j<n}a_j/\iota(j+1)$ from [[thm-linearity-of-the-integral]]; that is a routine consequence and is not stated as a separate claim.
