research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/cex-absolute-value-integrable-without-the-function.md-745-
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/cex-absolute-value-integrable-without-the-function.md-746-[L1] If $u$ is integrable on $[a,b]$ with values in $[m,M]$ and $\varphi$ is continuous on $[m,M]$, then $\varphi\circ u$ is integrable ([[thm-composition-with-a-continuous-function]]); an integrable function is bounded, so such $m$ and $M$ exist ([[def-darboux-sums]], [[def-bounded-set]], [[def-interval]]).
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/cex-absolute-value-integrable-without-the-function.md-747-
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/cex-absolute-value-integrable-without-the-function.md:748:[L2] Sums and scalar multiples of integrable functions are integrable, with $\int_a^b(\lambda u + \nu w) = \lambda\int_a^b u + \nu\int_a^b w$ ([[thm-linearity-of-the-integral]]).
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/cex-absolute-value-integrable-without-the-function.md-749-
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/cex-absolute-value-integrable-without-the-function.md-750-[L3] If $u \le w$ pointwise on $[a,b]$ and both are integrable then $\int_a^b u \le \int_a^b w$ ([[thm-monotonicity-of-the-integral]]).
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/cex-absolute-value-integrable-without-the-function.md-751-
--
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/cex-absolute-value-integrable-without-the-function.md-785-
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/cex-absolute-value-integrable-without-the-function.md-786-## Remarks
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/cex-absolute-value-integrable-without-the-function.md-787-
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/cex-absolute-value-integrable-without-the-function.md:788:- **Every integrability clause comes from one theorem plus linearity.** The only input that produces integrability is [[thm-composition-with-a-continuous-function]], with [[thm-linearity-of-the-integral]] recombining the pieces; claim 3 additionally uses [[thm-monotonicity-of-the-integral]], which is the one place an inequality between integrals is needed. The identities of [L5] are algebra, and they are what turns a statement about composing with $|\cdot|$ and $(\cdot)^2$ into statements about products and lattice operations. In particular no new estimate on Darboux sums is made here.
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/cex-absolute-value-integrable-without-the-function.md-789-
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/cex-absolute-value-integrable-without-the-function.md-790-- **The polarisation identity is used, and it is why $f^2$ comes first.** There is no direct route from integrability of $f$ and of $g$ to integrability of $fg$ through the composition theorem, because $(s,t) \mapsto st$ is a function of two variables and the theorem composes with one. Writing $fg$ through squares of sums and differences reduces it to the one-variable case.
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/cex-absolute-value-integrable-without-the-function.md-791-
--
