research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/fs-integration-by-parts-needs-no-integrability-hypothesis.md-2629-[L4] A product of two integrable functions on $[a,b]$ is integrable ([[cor-integrability-of-absolute-values-products-and-lattice-operations]], claim 1).
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/fs-integration-by-parts-needs-no-integrability-hypothesis.md-2630-
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/fs-integration-by-parts-needs-no-integrability-hypothesis.md:2631:[L5] Sums of integrable functions are integrable, and $\int_a^b(w_1+w_2) = \int_a^b w_1 + \int_a^b w_2$ ([[thm-linearity-of-the-integral]]).
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/fs-integration-by-parts-needs-no-integrability-hypothesis.md-2632-
research/audit/wave11-refuter-tasks/fs-integration-by-parts-needs-no-integrability-hypothesis.md-2633-[L6] If $H$ is differentiable at every point of $[a,b]$ with $H'$ integrable there, then $\int_a^b H' = H(b)-H(a)$ ([[thm-ftc-second-part]]).
