research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1462702-+{"id":"ex-zero-second-derivative-extremum-trichotomy","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_taylor.html","https://www-users.cse.umn.edu/~voronov/5615-20/pdfs/12-2-1.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources give the inconclusive zero-second-derivative warning and the higher-derivative test. The item packages the canonical x^4, -x^4, and x^3 witnesses and checks their derivatives and local order directly.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1462703-+{"id":"def-oriented-integral","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Integral_calculus"],"rationale":"The source states the same orientation convention: reversing the endpoints negates the integral and an equal-endpoint integral is zero. The item only binds that standard convention to the library's Darboux integral.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1462704-+{"id":"lem-integrability-on-a-subinterval","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_rintprop.html"],"rationale":"The source states that Riemann integrability on [a,b] implies integrability on every closed subinterval, with the same hypotheses and direction. The Darboux-criterion proof in the item is local.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log:1462705:+{"id":"thm-linearity-of-the-integral","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.math.cmu.edu/~gautam/c/2026-269/notes/rint.html","https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Integral_calculus"],"rationale":"The sources state closure under scalar multiplication and addition and the identical linearity identity. The repository supplies a local upper/lower-sum proof. Its statement is correct; the separate step-reference typo in proof step 4.2 is recorded in the findings.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1462706-+{"id":"thm-monotonicity-of-the-integral","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.math.cmu.edu/~gautam/c/2026-269/notes/rint.html","https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Integral_calculus"],"rationale":"The sources state that pointwise f<=g for two Riemann-integrable functions implies the same order between their integrals. The item extends it to oriented notation by a direct local case split.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1462707-+{"id":"thm-additivity-over-subintervals","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_rintprop.html","https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Integral_calculus"],"rationale":"The sources give additivity for an ordered interior split and the subinterval integrability equivalence. The item packages the result for every third point and every endpoint order using the oriented-integral convention.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1462708-+{"id":"lem-changing-a-function-at-finitely-many-points","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_rintprop.html"],"rationale":"The source states that changing a bounded function at finitely many points preserves Riemann integrability and the integral. The item's oscillation-budget proof is local.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
